Keep Cairo data draw targets byte-order safe on big-endian.

On big-endian, cairo_image_surface_create_for_data(..., ARGB32, ...)
expects native ARGB/XRGB bytes.  Mozilla's long-standing SPARC compatibility
patch keeps A8R8G8B8_UINT32 mapped to BGRA/BGRX, so callers can still hand
Cairo a BGRA/BGRX data buffer.  Letting Cairo draw directly into that buffer
stores native ARGB bytes where the owner later reads BGRA, corrupting CSS
gradients, SVG rasterization, text alpha, and other intermediate surfaces.

For these data draw targets, draw into a native Cairo image surface and swizzle
back to the caller's buffer when the target is flushed or destroyed.  This keeps
the public buffer format unchanged while letting Cairo use the byte order it
requires internally.

Also normalize native Cairo source surfaces when wrapping them as ImageBitmap
images, so drawSnapshot/tab-preview readback does not hand canvas image drawing
an unsupported native-endian surface.

The byte-order conversions in this patch use the compiler's __BYTE_ORDER__
condition.  The backend selection, texture-client format policy, and flush
hooks are Solaris/SPARC-only because they match the supported Userland runtime
path.  Mechanical signature changes that pass the existing uninitialized flag
through Cairo are unguarded support code.

Dependency: apply after SPARC-01-cairo-surface-format.patch. This patch relies
on the native Cairo surface formats introduced there and provides the data
target bridge used by later shared-image, blob, and SWGL boundary fixes.

